hi this is Mario with Mario's math  tutoring coming to you with another math  video to help you boost your math score  improve your understanding and hopefully  make math more fun and less stressful so  in this video we're going to talk about  is how to calculate standard deviation  by hand okay so you can do this on your  calculator and I might do a video on  that but for this video we're gonna talk  about how to actually calculate standard  deviation by hand so first of all what  is standard deviation the standard  deviation gives you an idea about how  spread out the data points are from the  mean okay from the average larger the  standard deviation the more the data  points are spread out the smaller the  standard deviation the closer the data  points are grouped together so that  gives you an overview about standard  deviation but here's the form that we're  going to be using so Sigma the Greek  letter here Sigma tells you what the  standard deviation is X sub I is each  one of our individual data points X bar  okay this means the mean or the average  okay have your data and is the number of  data points okay that you're working  with okay and and then this Sigma Greek  letter Sigma here is the sum okay so  this is your finding the sum of all the  data points okay well in this case  you're finding the sum of all the  deviations squared okay so let's go  ahead and do an example so find the  standard deviation of the following quiz  scores five eight eight nine and ten  okay so just imagine that you had five  people they took a quiz let's say it was  a 10 point quiz these are their scores  somebody did great somebody did not so  great but you want to just get an idea  about what the standard deviation is  this is a small amount of data just to  illustrate how to go through and do this  so what I would do this is how I  normally do it is I make a table okay so  here's our table looks something like  this okay so I'm going to list all the  data points which are five eight eight  nine and ten okay then we're going to  take each data point that's these here  minus the mean okay and then what we're  going to do is we're going to take each  of those standard deviations do  means like how far off from the mean you  are and we're gonna square them okay  then we're gonna add them all together  okay so we're gonna take that sum we're  gonna then divide it by how many data  points we have okay which is N and then  we're gonna take the square root and  that'll give us our standard deviation  okay so you're with me so far okay so  first thing we need to do is find the  mean okay this x-bar that's the average  so you probably already know how to find  the average you just add up all the data  points so that's five plus eight plus  eight plus nine plus ten all divided by  five data points so this comes out to 40  divided by five which equals eight so  our average score is eight so what we're  going to do is we're going to take five  minus 8 which is negative 3 8 minus 8  which is 0 8 minus 8 which is 0 9 minus  8 which is 1 and 10 minus 8 which is 2  so those are the deviations we're gonna  then square them negative 3 squared is  positive 9 0 squared is 0 0 squared is 0  1 squared is 1 and 2 squared is 4 we're  gonna add those up so that comes out to  14 we're gonna divide by how many data  points we have which in this case it's 5  okay and then what we're going to do so  this is 14 divided by 5 and we're going  to take the square root and if we do  that we get approximately one point six  seven so that's our standard deviation  and it gives us a sense of how spread  out the data points are from the mean  now the more data points we have you  know it would give us a little bit more  information we have a limited amount of  data here but just to you know  illustrate how the process works when  you're doing it by hand  so I hope you're enjoying these videos  go ahead and subscribe to the channel if  you have comments go ahead and put the  c